;; talk/private--replies.scm — the private surface's author answers the objections in talk/private--*.scm.

;; ── canon ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(reply 'r-private-canon-1 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-canon-1 :stance 'accept
  :because (canon 'htlc-constants-are-simnet "Right: the default payment was assumed to carry real money on an EVM mainnet, and the queue says it cannot yet. Added d-private-pay-kind for the maintainer, with three live answers: held payments and paying others waits, unconditional payments through the person's own company meanwhile, or no real money on an EVM mainnet on this surface. The default now says what happens before the deadlines are decided."))

(reply 'r-private-canon-2 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-canon-2 :stance 'accept
  :because (canon 'collateral-secured "Right on both counts: one word must not mean two things across faces, and my basis was a paraphrase, not evidence. d-private-what-is-certain now depends on d-kernel-secured and only asks how this face shows the cost of leaving; the leaning moved to counting the locked share as for sure and showing the cost of leaving on the company's screen. Counting it as promised stays as an option for the maintainer, not as my leaning."))

(reply 'r-private-canon-3 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-canon-3 :stance 'amend
  :because (protocol "core/protocol/dispute/lending-covenant.ts" "The late window is not a missing figure for savings: the protocol rejects any savings agreement whose late window is not zero, so showing it would show a zero that always holds. The rest stands. The savings screen now offers leaving, its question asks how the person gets the money back, and the save flow ends by saying who holds authority (only the person asks to close, the company pays out, and the company may change the rate on savings that can be taken back at any time, for the future only), and that leaving the company returns the principal and what it has earned, frozen when leaving starts, as money the company owes beyond what is locked."))

(reply 'r-private-canon-4 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-canon-4 :stance 'amend
  :because (canon 'recovery-coverage-visible "A failed copy that never reaches the person is a swallowed failure; agreed. But no field of a core type states coverage, so a screen cannot list it among its figures; the kernel's k-evidence states covered, accepted or failed per company and chain, and the new-phone and join flows now use it. Added d-private-coverage-shown, which leans toward marking the first screen whenever a copy failed or the chain has no service, so the failure reaches the place where 'for sure' is claimed, not only the protection screen."))

(reply 'r-private-canon-5 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-canon-5 :stance 'amend
  :because (guess "Two questions were folded into one. What a private surface leaves out is a question of what the product is, which is the user's, and it now depends on d-kernel-how-many. Whether the queue's Pro switch must exist here is repository canon, the maintainer's, so it is now d-private-pro-toggle with its own options. I do not agree that hiding actions conflicts with the switch: the queue item asks for detail behind a switch, and the leaning puts this surface's hidden figures there while keeping left-out actions out."))

(reply 'r-private-canon-6 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-canon-6 :stance 'accept
  :because (canon 'launch-flows "Right: the gap pointed at nobody and the file never said what holds on TRON. The gap now names the borrowing app proposed in apps.scm under d-apps-borrowing and says the Lending flow has no borrower here if that is rejected. Added d-private-tron for the maintainer: on native TRON the watching service cannot answer, and the leaning offers real money there with 'not covered' said at joining and a longer time to answer under d-private-response-time."))

;; ── person ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(reply 'r-private-person-1 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-person-1 :stance 'amend
  :because (guess "Right that the first payment is the worst moment to ask: the recipient is not looking, and the payment fails before they are asked. The leaning moved to asking when the person joins a company, the one moment they are always present, and a new option asks when they request a stated amount. Wrong that the question breaks the cannot-list: the person may not judge a company's health, but they may pick a number when told what it can cost at worst, and the question states the amount as the most they could lose with that company."))

(reply 'r-private-person-2 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-person-2 :stance 'accept
  :because (canon 'auto-top-up-default "The surface contradicted its own rule. d-private-seeded-settings is now only about this setting, and leans toward switching it off in the signature right after opening and offering it, with its trigger and fee cap, at the moment the person lets the company owe more than the trigger, which is the only moment it could start to cost them and the moment they are present."))

(reply 'r-private-person-3 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-person-3 :stance 'amend
  :because (guess "Right that the copy is useless without proof that the new phone is the same owner: the protocol derives the identity from one secret phrase. Added d-private-restore-key, leaning toward showing and checking the words when money first arrives, and a need for the kernel to restore from them. Right that the person must learn whether the last copy was accepted: see d-private-coverage-shown. On asking: the permission for the service to answer for the person is authority handed to a third party, so it is now asked at joining. The coded copy itself hands nobody any power, and I still lean toward having it on and named rather than asked; the critic's position is kept as the option both-asked in d-private-copy-consent, for the user."))

(reply 'r-private-person-4 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-person-4 :stance 'amend
  :because (canon 'exit-alone "Right that the person cannot tell a company that is restarting from one that is gone, so a question that depends on telling them apart is unfair; that is now in the person's cannot-list. The question now comes only when waiting can cost them: money owed with nothing locked, or a deadline that runs out. Someone whose money is all locked loses nothing by waiting and is not asked. Right too that finishing must not need them at a set time: a new default finishes a started leaving without asking the first time the app runs after the window ends, within a chain cost stated when leaving was started."))

(reply 'r-private-person-5 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-person-5 :stance 'accept
  :because (guess "A headline that drops on a busy day while nothing happened to the money cannot be trusted. Together with o-private-canon-2 this moved the leaning: the locked share is counted as for sure in its fixed protocol meaning, and the cost of leaving, which does move with the chain's price, is shown where leaving is decided."))

(reply 'r-private-person-6 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-person-6 :stance 'accept
  :because (guess "The person had no way to look back. Added the job private-what-happened, d-private-history (leaning toward their own exportable record, with a checkable proof as the alternative), a history screen and a flow that uses the kernel's record and payment notes. The kernel's record does not yet list this person's need; that is said in a new need."))

;; ── protocol ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(reply 'r-private-protocol-1 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-protocol-1 :stance 'accept
  :because (protocol "core/account/tx/handlers/balance/set-credit-limit.ts" "Checked: the proposer sets the line it grants the other side, and nothing asks the other side to accept. Room to send then includes borrowed room that the company can raise alone. Room to send is gone from the first screen; the company's screen shows the line it lent; and d-private-spend-borrowed leans toward payments stopping at the person's own money, which needs a new figure from the runtime: what can be sent without owing."))

(reply 'r-private-protocol-2 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-protocol-2 :stance 'amend
  :because (protocol "core/account/tx/handlers/rebalance/request-collateral.ts" "The contradiction is real and is fixed under d-private-seeded-settings. One part is overstated: an open request cannot be changed or repeated until the chain confirms the locking or the company refunds the fee, so a new fee is taken only after the last one was used or returned, not again and again while the company sits on a request. The fee is still taken at the moment of asking, which is why the setting is now off until the person turns it on with the fee cap in front of them."))

(reply 'r-private-protocol-3 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-protocol-3 :stance 'accept
  :because (protocol "core/watchtower/last-resort-appointment.ts" "Checked: the watching service can be appointed to answer a dispute the owner cannot answer, holding the latest proof and an authorization bound to one account and window, with no power to spend. A wake sent to a lost phone protects nobody. d-private-evidence now has that option and leans toward it, and states that on native TRON the service cannot submit at all."))

(reply 'r-private-protocol-4 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-protocol-4 :stance 'amend
  :because (canon 'keep-evidence "Agreed that 'for sure' is only true while the latest signed state is kept and someone answers in time. The fix is not to change the word, which keeps the shared meaning, but to say so where it is claimed when that condition fails: d-private-coverage-shown leans toward the first screen saying an amount is not backed whenever its copy failed or its chain has no service. Coverage has no figure in the core types, so that statement comes from the kernel's k-evidence, not from a figure on the screen."))

(reply 'r-private-protocol-5 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-protocol-5 :stance 'accept
  :because (protocol "core/entity/tx/handlers/htlc/payment.ts" "Instant fails a recipient whose phone is asleep, and the get-paid job depends on it. Added d-private-pay-speed with the cost of each choice stated; the leaning picks by recipient: instant when what they handed over says they are online, the slow kind otherwise. That needs the handle to say so, which is a new need."))

(reply 'r-private-protocol-6 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-protocol-6 :stance 'accept
  :because (canon 'collateral-split "Claiming moves the held amount to the person's side, and beyond the company's locked money it rests on the company's word. The risk was accepted when the person set the amount, but it is still theirs, so the default is now labelled holder and its value says what lands where."))

;; ── structure ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(reply 'r-private-structure-1 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-structure-1 :stance 'amend
  :because (protocol "core/account/tx/handlers/rebalance/request-collateral.ts" "The decision now depends on d-private-who, and the option the protocol forbids (the surface allowing a small amount by itself) is gone. Holding money at an address is not enough to pay the company to lock first: the fee is taken from the person's room to send in that account, so it is open only to a holder who has already brought money in. That option is kept with that condition. The leaning moved to asking at joining, which works whichever way d-private-who is settled."))

(reply 'r-private-structure-2 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-structure-2 :stance 'accept
  :because (guess "One decision carried three settings and argued one. d-private-seeded-settings now covers only the automatic request to lock money; listing is d-private-listed and the time to answer is d-private-response-time, each with its own options, reason and changes-if, and each default now follows from its own decision."))

(reply 'r-private-structure-3 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-structure-3 :stance 'accept
  :because (canon 'credit-secured-unsecured "Both halves hold. The meaning of 'for sure' is now settled first, in d-kernel-secured, and the first screen depends on it; d-private-what-is-certain comes after and only decides how this face shows the cost of leaving. The grouping that was made silently in the figure list is now d-private-home-grouping, and d-private-holder-named moved to naming companies only beside the promised part on the first screen, so the leanings no longer add up to a grid."))

(reply 'r-private-structure-4 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-structure-4 :stance 'amend
  :because (guess "Accepted for the defaults with real alternatives: d-private-pay-speed, d-private-pay-kind, d-private-which-company and d-private-exchange-margin are new decisions, and the speed default's risk is now the holder's. Not for the payment limit: the protocol already makes the payer set the most a payment may cost, and a cap above the quoted total would take a fee the person never saw, which d-private-consent already forbids, so it has no live alternative."))

(reply 'r-private-structure-5 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-structure-5 :stance 'accept
  :because (guess "The argument supported naming the chain where it decides something, and that option was missing. It is now an option and the leaning: named when paying, being paid, bringing in, sending out and changing, and beside an amount only where the person holds that currency on more than one chain. The changes-if now tests that claim."))

(reply 'r-private-structure-6 :by "opus-private" :to 'o-private-structure-6 :stance 'accept
  :because (guess "d-private-consent now depends on d-kernel-consent and chooses among the kernel's kinds, not a narrower list of its own. The straw-man options are replaced by three that a sensible face could pick. The leaning includes 'publishes something about me', which is why listing now has its own decision. The because no longer claims the settlement law covers every action; it says this face extends that line by choice."))
